# Exercise 1: Implementing the Singleton Pattern

## Objective

- Understand the Singleton Design Pattern.
- Implement a Singleton class in Java.
- Ensure that only one instance of the `Logger` class exists throughout the application.
- Verify the Singleton implementation using a test class.

---

## Prerequisites

- Java Development Kit (JDK 8 or later)
- Eclipse IDE / IntelliJ IDEA / VS Code
- Basic knowledge of Java classes and objects

---

# Step 1: Create a New Java Project

### Project Name

```text
SingletonPatternExample
```

### Procedure

1. Open your Java IDE.
2. Create a new Java Project.
3. Name the project **SingletonPatternExample**.
4. Click **Finish**.

---

# Step 2: Create the `Logger` Class

### File Name

```text
Logger.java
```

### Code

```java
public class Logger {

    // Create a private static instance
    private static Logger instance;

    // Private constructor
    private Logger() {
        System.out.println("Logger Instance Created");
    }

    // Public method to return the single instance
    public static Logger getInstance() {

        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new Logger();
        }

        return instance;
    }

    // Logging method
    public void log(String message) {
        System.out.println("Log: " + message);
    }
}
```

---

# Step 3: Create the Test Class

### File Name

```text
SingletonTest.java
```

### Code

```java
public class SingletonTest {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Logger logger1 = Logger.getInstance();

        Logger logger2 = Logger.getInstance();

        logger1.log("Application Started");

        logger2.log("Application Running");

        if (logger1 == logger2) {
            System.out.println("Only one Logger instance exists.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Multiple Logger instances exist.");
        }

    }

}
```

